    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A population of cicadas is modeled by a function defined by \\(f(w) = 250 \\boldcdot e^{(0.5w)}\\) where \\(w\\) is the number of weeks since the population was first measured.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Explain why solving the equation \\(500 = 250 \\boldcdot e^{(0.5w)}\\) gives the number of weeks it takes for the cicada population to double.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How many weeks does it take the cicada population to double? Show your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use graphing technology to graph \\(y=f(w)\\) and \\(y = 100,\\!000\\) on the same axes. Explain why we can use the intersection of the two graphs to estimate when the cicada population will reach 100,000.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can use the natural logarithm to solve exponential equations that are expressed with the base \\(e\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose a bacteria population is modeled by the equation \\(f(h) = 1,\\!000 \\boldcdot e^{(0.5h)}\\), where \\(h\\) is the number of hours since the population was first measured. When will the population reach 500,000?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "One way to answer this is to solve the equation \\(1,\\!000 \\boldcdot e^{(0.5h)} = 500,\\!000\\), which is when \\(e^{(0.5h)} = 500\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The natural logarithm tells us the exponent to which we raise \\(e\\) to get a given number, so \\(0.5h =\\ln 500\\). This means \\(h = \\frac{\\ln 500}{0.5}\\) or about 12.4, so it takes 12.4 hours (or 12 hours and 24 minutes) for the population…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can also use a graph to solve an exponential equation. To solve \\(1,\\!000 \\boldcdot e^{(0.5h)} = 500,\\!000\\), we can graph \\(y = 1,\\!000 \\boldcdot e^{(0.5h)}\\) and \\(y=500,\\!000\\) on the same coordinate plane and find the point of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L15-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "15.3: Cicada Population",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The graph shows us that the bacteria population reaches 500,000 when the input value is a little over 12, or about 12 hours after the population was first measured.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
